 # Neural Parametric Gaussians
-
-
 ## Overview
 Neural Parametric Gaussians (NPG) is a two-stage method for monocular non-rigid object reconstruction that imposes a coarse deformation model as regularization for fine-level 3D Gaussian optimization [S1]. Unlike concurrent works that apply neural networks directly to all Gaussians, NPG uses a network only to drive an initial coarse point template, while the Gaussians themselves are optimized with static parameters that are deformed by the coarse model [S1]. This approach avoids heavy runtime increases for large scenes and ensures temporal consistency in novel views [S1].
-
 ## How it works
 NPG operates in two stages: first, a coarse neural parametric point model is fitted to monocular video sequences using a low-rank deformation basis; second, 3D Gaussians are initialized and optimized in local volumes defined by the point sets, where Gaussian parameters are shared over time and deformation is driven by the coarse point set [S1].
